  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 213 PM MDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nBeginning Sunday, the Tri-State area is forecast to be under a \nmostly zonal upper-level flow with a ridge building west of the \nRocky Mountains. High temperatures are forecast to remain \nunseasonably warm for the county warning area (CWA) through Tuesday \nwith highs in the 90s to 100s. A weak cold front moving through \nTuesday night will bring modest cooling, but high temperatures are \nstill forecast in the 90s for Wednesday. \n\nStarting Monday evening through at least Wednesday evening, there is \na chance for showers and thunderstorms overnight due to embedded \nshortwave passages. Currently, Wednesday is looking like our best \nchance with Probability of Precipitation (PoPs) from 30-50% for the \nCWA. \n\nDespite the unseasonably warm temperatures, fire weather concerns \nare minimal for the forecast period. Relative humidity (RH) values \nfor the western CWA are forecast in the low teens to 20s for Sunday \nand Monday. Peak wind gusts are forecast to be around 25 mph, but \nhighest wind gusts do not coincide with lowest RH values of the day. \nBeginning Monday afternoon, winds will primarily be from the east \nadvecting more moisture into the Tri-State area allowing minimum RH \nvalues to gradually recover into the 20s to 30s after Tuesday.",
